# Local rules for tools/scan-secrets.sh
#
# Copy to .secret-scan.local, which is untracked and must stay that way.
#
# This file exists because the two most useful checks cannot live in a public
# repository. A denylist naming the internal systems a sample was derived from
# would disclose them. And a check for a specific credential needs a way to
# recognize that credential without storing it.
#
# Two directives:
#
#   pattern:<extended regex>   matched case-insensitively against every file
#   fingerprint:<sha256 hex>   matched against the SHA-256 of every token of
#                              20 or more characters found in any file
#
# --- recognizing a real credential -------------------------------------------
#
# Add a fingerprint without the value passing through your shell history, a
# command line, or a file:
#
#   read -rs SECRET && printf '%s' "$SECRET" | shasum -a 256 \
#     | awk '{print "fingerprint:" $1}' >> .secret-scan.local; unset SECRET
#
# A fingerprint is a one-way hash, so this file does not carry the secret. Keep
# it untracked regardless: a hash still confirms a guess.
#
# --- project terms -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Add anything that would identify the origin of this work: organization and team
# names, internal repository or service names, product code names, ticket
# prefixes, internal hostnames and wiki paths, specific private CIDR ranges,
# domain-specific identifier schemes, personal names, staff numbers, and named
# credential profiles.
#
# Example shape, not real values:
#
#   pattern:\b(acme|acme-platform|projectfalcon)\b
#   pattern:\b(10\.42\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3})\b
#   pattern:\b[A-Z][0-9]{6}\b
